A||||30||Mr.Frosty||What can you do with those crappy sail panels?||||5||
Z||000000||Mr.Frosty||03-22-2001||01:12 PM||||Does anybody have any good ideas about what to do when your sail panels deteriorate?It will happen to all of our sail panels eventually.I don't want to hear "junkyards" or "this guy I know can get you used..."I think someone needs to start thinking about manufacturing a replacement panel for the notchback.Maybe something that can be color matched to the car(just a thought).I always see those custom fastback panels that are being made.Why not notchback?It seems the notchback owners are getting the short end. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>88 Formula T-top <BR>85 GT Turbo||||reg||5
Z||000001||Sevaan||03-22-2001||01:17 PM||||I agree. What cool things could put put thereto replace the panels? Both of mine are damaged an dI would like to fix them up.||||reg||14
Z||000002||GoCartGuy||03-22-2001||01:19 PM||||Paul McKibben makes them.  Here's the link to his site.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/biz/fierosails/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/biz/fierosails/</A> <P>------------------<BR>'85 GT "The GoCart"||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadMan||03-22-2001||01:39 PM||||What pi$$es me off is that the black plastic surrounding my sail panels is fading to gray.  I could paint them, I suppose, but what kind of paint should I use that would stand up to weather and adhere to the plastic well?||||reg||5
Z||000004||Mr.Frosty||03-22-2001||01:45 PM||||thanks gocartguy,that's a cool site.I'm probably going to get some since they're inexpensive.I've repainted the trim around the panels more times than I can remember,but I've used satin black,gloss black enamel in a can(krylon?)It doesn't last but a couple of seasons.I am going to have a shop match them to the body of the car then put new balck lenses in I guess.I am sick of the cracked,seperated,faded sail panels.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>------------------<BR>88 Formula T-top <BR>85 GT Turbo||||reg||8
Z||000005||WKDFIRO||03-22-2001||01:46 PM||||Ive been going through the couch cushions looking for nickels and dimes to buy a pair of GTU sail panels from Paul McKibben, compared to the GT sail panels they are a GREAT price!||||reg||14
Z||000006||Mr.Frosty||03-22-2001||01:53 PM||||Has anyone used these inserts yet?If so are they decent?Does it have the right curve to it,or does the sticky tape do that part?<P>------------------<BR>88 Formula T-top <BR>85 GT Turbo||||reg||5
Z||000007||Oreif||03-22-2001||02:24 PM||||The plastic insert ones have had problems with the tape loosening. If it happens right away, Paul will send you another at no cost. For notchback cars, I prefer to go to a junkyard and get the 1984 sails. In 1984 the sails were glass. These work on all years of notchback Fiero's. The only one you would have a problem with is the 85 GT since there were no GT's made in 1984. But you can still get the blank Glass or the SE version.<BR>I installed the glass ones on my car a few years back. One other advantage of using the glass ones is that the outside frame  and the part that holds the little emblem have a rubberized coating. (As opposed to the allplastic from 85 thru 88) There are many Latex cleaners and polishes to clean them up to look brand new. (the cleaners/polishes can be bought at places that sell latex clothing, little kinky for me but hey they only charge $1.98 for a small bottle) Also the little emblem is available too. Finally If you really want a custom set, most glass places will cut you an identical piece and curve it. It is expensive.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 03-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Alex4mula||03-22-2001||02:58 PM||||I recommend Paul McKibben sails 100% to anyone. Don't look anywhere else. They are exactly like the originals with the curvature and all. You can't tell the diff. I installed them 2 week ago when I got my car back from the body shop (new paint). Just take the whole assembly out like Paul suggest. It is very easy and more convenient to take out the panels (pain in a$$). Clean them very well and paint them with plastic trim paint. Also I suggest you to buy the Pontiac triangle logos and replace those too. The panel will look like new. See my car at Daytona to convince yourself.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tigger||03-22-2001||03:05 PM||||The Krylon might be the root of your problem.  From my own experience, Krylon seems to fade over time in the weather if it's not protected with some sort of armorall or son-of-a-gun.  Even protected, it still wants to fade.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Mr.Frosty||03-22-2001||05:10 PM||||Yeah I'm not going that route anymore.I'm thinking about shaving off the chevron(emblem) location before I repaint the holder though.I don't know whether I like the look of them.I bought new ones a while back and one fell out,even though I used adhesive.I read somewhere in archives about someone doing this with a sander(carefully).I am debating whether to change the color of them too.Hmmm Red holder\black glass or Black holder\red glass....I am going to take them to a shop and have the correct shade applied with clearcoat for longevity.<P>------------------<BR>88 Formula T-top <BR>85 GT Turbo||||reg||11
Z||000011||sqoach||03-22-2001||05:20 PM||||i wish there was a way to just get rid of them. like glassing it all in to match the body color or something. i bought one at a junkyard a while back and refurbished it, just haven't put it on. i also noticed that they rattle when my bass hits  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.JPG">  <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)||||reg||1
Z||000012||Fierowrecker||03-22-2001||06:06 PM||||You heard of Paul McKibbon...<BR>But for the paint, I use NAPA trim paint, satin (semi-gloss)...<BR>I did the mirrors and other trim last year and it still looks great!||||reg||1
