Z||000014||MrPBody||03-22-2001||09:04 PM||||Here's mine without emblems.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sailpanel01.jpg"> <P>Paint is Mar-Hyde satin black trim paint.  (Look for a Fiero on the can.)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||FieroBUZZ||03-22-2001||09:10 PM||||I've got a cracked one and one with lots of small cracks.  I've been designing a panel to replace the smoked glass. It will have a scoop built in to funnel cooling air thru the sail into the engine compartment.  I'll put up some pics when I get to the stage where it looks like something.  Picture one of those 'lazy C" scoops but thru the sail rather than wrapped around it.<BR>Gary||||reg||1
Z||000016||FieroJoe||03-22-2001||09:25 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mr.Frosty:<BR><B>I am going to take them to a shop and have the correct shade applied with clearcoat for longevity.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I have refinished mine.  The first time I repainted the plastic surround, I used just Ace Premium Enamel.  It sucks, dont ever get it!  Faded, scratched easily.  This time, I redid all my trim in ace Rust Stop Satin Black (rust stop is the best adhering paint I have ever used from a can)  I also primed with rust stop primer before hand.  For the finish coat I used a high gloss polyurethane spray.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
