Z||000026||Fierowrecker||03-25-2001||10:06 AM||||Hey MrP!<BR>What color gold is that?<BR>It looks like California gold to me...<BR>Same as my wifes Fiero...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000027||MrPBody||03-25-2001||08:55 PM||||Fierowrecker --<P>"California Gold"?  I remember that . . .  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Nason calls it "Trans-Am Yellow".  I don't think Trans-Ams came from the factory painted that color; however, I remember a <I>very</I> fast Camaro racing in the Trans-Am series, painted a similar yellow.  I think Carl Schaefer (sp?) was the driver.<P>You could also call it School Bus Yellow.  I think I like "California Gold" better.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000028||ToddAsh||03-26-2001||11:03 PM|||| How do you get the sail panels off. I'm junking a '84 and want to keep them for my '85. Thanks,Todd||||reg||1
Z||000029||theogre||03-26-2001||11:18 PM||||to get the 84 ones off you have to pull the interior trim and then you can get to the nuts that hold the sails.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000030||Tigger||03-27-2001||12:32 AM||||Also remove that triangle panel on the inboard side.  It's a sort of a pain, but it makes it easier to get to the clips that hold the sail panel from the backside.  Bend the tabs on the clips just enough to release the sail panel.  I found this way you don't have to pry on the sail from the outside.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 03-27-2001).]||||reg||1
